      "rationale": "The report shows that sqlparse.format(sql, strip_whitespace=True) is destroying necessary single spaces (e.g. \"select (select 2)\" becomes \"select(select2)\"); tracing this to StripWhitespaceFilter._stripws_default in sqlparse/filters/others.py shows its ternary is inverted (`' ' if last_was_ws or is_first_char else ''` instead of the correct `'' if last_was_ws or is_first_char else ' '`), so every lone, needed whitespace token is wiped out instead of leading/duplicate whitespace. The expected values in the tests come directly from the bug report's own assertion (`'select (select 2)'`) and from sqlparse's documented intent that strip_whitespace collapses redundant/leading/trailing whitespace while preserving single meaningful separators, which is evident from the surrounding logic (`is_first_char`/`last_was_ws` tracking) and the top-level `process()` which explicitly strips a trailing whitespace token at depth 0.",
